Approach 1: Brute Force
  Algorithm

In this approach, I first create a separate union array to store the final result.

I traverse the first sorted array and insert only those elements that are greater than their previous element. Since the array is sorted, this removes duplicate values while copying the elements.

After that, I traverse the second array. For every element, I perform a linear search in the union array.

If the element is already present, I skip it.
Otherwise, I insert it into the union array.

This approach works correctly, but every element of the second array requires searching through the union array, making it inefficient for larger inputs.

  Time Complexity
Traversing the first array → O(n)
Traversing the second array → O(m)
Linear search for every element of the second array → O(n + m) (maximum size of union array)

Overall Time Complexity: O((n + m) × m) (approximately O(n × m))

Space Complexity

A separate union array is used to store the answer.

Space Complexity: O(n + m)

 Approach 2: Better Approach (Binary Search)
 Algorithm

The idea is almost the same as the brute-force approach.

First, copy the unique elements of the first array into the union array.

Instead of performing a linear search for every element of the second array, use Binary Search because the union array remains sorted.

If Binary Search finds the element, skip it.
Otherwise, insert it into the union array.

Replacing linear search with binary search reduces the searching time and makes this approach faster than brute force.

 Time Complexity
Traverse first array → O(n)
Binary Search for every element of second array → O(m log n)

Overall Time Complexity: O(n + m log n)

 Space Complexity

A separate union array is required.

Space Complexity: O(n + m)

  Approach 3: Optimal Approach (Two Pointer)
  Algorithm

Since both arrays are already sorted, there is no need to search for elements repeatedly.

Two pointers are used:

first points to the first array.
second points to the second array.

Both pointers move simultaneously.

If both elements are equal, insert the value only once into the union array and move both pointers.
If the first array contains the smaller element, insert it and move only the first pointer.
If the second array contains the smaller element, insert it and move only the second pointer.

Before inserting any element, compare it with the last inserted element of the union array. If both values are the same, skip the insertion to avoid duplicates.

Once one array is completely traversed, simply traverse the remaining elements of the other array and continue applying the same duplicate check.

Since each pointer moves only forward and every element is processed only once, this is the most efficient solution.

 Time Complexity

Each element from both arrays is visited only once.

Overall Time Complexity: O(n + m)

  Space Complexity

The union array stores the final result.

Space Complexity: O(n + m)